#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:203
msgid "Complexities with GS handling on context switches"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:206
msgid "History"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:208
msgid ""
"In 32-bit, data segments need reloading on entry to the kernel, and "
"restoring on exit to userspace.  Only the segment selector is necessary, as "
"all segment data resides in the GDT/LDT.  Bases in the GDT/LDT are 32 bits "
"wide.  The segment selector values are user-chosen, and effectively "
"arbitrary."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:213
msgid ""
"The 32-bit mechanism is slow, so in 64-bit, segments were made mostly flat "
"so as to not need reloading on entry/exit.  FS and GS segment bases were "
"extended to 64 bits, and became accessible via MSRs. Also, a separate "
"GS_SHADOW value was introduced.  The SWAPGS instruction swaps GS_BASE and "
"GS_SHADOW, as the only action needed on entry/exit."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:219
msgid ""
"64-bit userspace needed to make the prctl() ARCH_SET_GS have a base value "
"greater than 32 bits, and a side effect of this syscall was to zero the GS "
"selector."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:223
msgid ""
"Then the FSGSBASE instructions came along, and userspace could finally "
"choose an arbitrary base address not previously registered via the syscall.  "
"Linux's ABI promises to preserve both the selector value and the full base, "
"even when they are disconnected."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:228
msgid ""
"When looking at the hardware capabilities, there are multiple x86 "
"instructions which modify GS:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:231
msgid "SWAPGS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:233
msgid ""
"Swaps the value in MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE with the active GS.base in the hidden "
"portion of the GS selector register."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:236
msgid "MOV <segment selector>, GS"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:238
msgid ""
"(legacy path, non-FRED) Loads GS with the selector specified in <segment "
"selector> and fetches the GS descriptor attributes, limit and base from the "
"GDT/LDT.  Writes a 32-bit base into the active GS.base, zero-extending it "
"into the 64-bit base register. It does not touch MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:243
msgid ""
"The problem with this is that because it writes the *current* GS.base, it "
"corrupts the active kernel per-CPU pointer (in %gs)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:246
msgid "LKGS <selector>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:248
msgid ""
"(FRED path, replaces MOV GS) Like MOV GS in that it loads the selector and "
"descriptor attributes, but it redirects the base write - instead of updating "
"the active GS.base, it writes the descriptor base into IA32_KERNEL_GS_BASE "
"(i.e. MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:253
msgid ""
"Critical caveat: it only writes a zero-extended 32-bit value, because GDT/"
"LDT descriptors only encode 32-bit bases. This means it cannot correctly "
"represent a full 64-bit user-space GS base (e.g. a TLS pointer), so a full "
"64-bit WRMSR is still required afterwards."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:258
msgid ""
"This instruction ensures that the kernel's per-CPU pointer stays good, and "
"does not need custom error handling."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:261
msgid ""
"MOV GS and LKGS are the only way to update the other fields of the GS "
"descriptor."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:264
msgid "WRGSBASE <reg>"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:266
msgid ""
"In 64-bit mode, it writes a full 64-bit value directly into the currently "
"active GS.base as FS.base and GS.base in 64-bit mode are expanded to 64-bit "
"to cover the full address space."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:270
msgid ""
"The problem in kernel context: the currently active GS.base belongs to the "
"kernel, not the user task. So using this during context switching would "
"corrupt the kernel's own GS.base, unless surrounded by SWAPGS (only safe in "
"IDT mode)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:275
msgid ""
"In the remaining modes, the upper 32 bits of the base are cleared instead."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:277
msgid "WRMSR MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE / WRMSRNS MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:279
msgid ""
"Writes a full 64-bit value into MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE, which holds the inactive "
"(user-space) GS.base - the one that gets swapped into the active GS.base on "
"SWAPGS. This is the only instruction that can correctly set a 64-bit user GS."
"base during a context switch from kernel mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:284
msgid ""
"The non-serializing nature of the write was accomplished by the two vendors "
"differently. AMD, starting with Zen4, made it the default through:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:287
msgid ""
"CPUID_Fn80000021_EAX [Extended Feature 2 EAX] (Core::X86::Cpuid::"
"FeatureExt2Eax)[1], FsGsKernelGsBaseNonSerializing which is fixed to 1"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:291
msgid ""
"and Intel through the WRMSRNS instruction which is the non-serializing "
"variant."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../arch/x86/x86_64/fsgs.rst:294
msgid ""
"Btw, while running in kernel mode, MSR_KERNEL_GS_BASE contains actually the "
"*user* GS.base. Thus, the naming can be confusing. Unless one thinks of it "
"as the kernel's access to GS.base as MSRs are accessible only in CPL0."
msgstr ""
